When you apply a CSS filter (like blur) directly on a container, all of its children inherit that rendering effect. That means if you simply add the locker icon inside the same element, it will also be blurred.

To have only the “background” look blurred while keeping the locker icon (and any other content you want sharp) clear, you need to separate the blurred “background” from the sharp foreground content. One common approach is to use an extra (or pseudo‑) element that covers the background and has the blur applied, while the actual content (including your icon) sits above it.

Below are two solutions.

──────────────────────────────
Solution 1: Restructure the HTML

Wrap the card’s background in an extra element. For example:

--------------------------------------------------
HTML:
--------------------------------------------------
<div class="info-card locked-card" id="card7">
  <div class="background-blur"></div>
  <div class="content">
    <div class="title">Locked</div>
    <div class="value">99</div>
    <div class="icon">🔒</div>
  </div>
</div>

--------------------------------------------------
CSS:
--------------------------------------------------
.locked-card {
  position: relative;
  overflow: hidden; /* ensure the absolute element doesn’t spill out */
}

.background-blur {
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
  filter: blur(6px);
  z-index: 1; /* behind the content */
}

.content {
  position: relative;
  z-index: 2;
  /* Add any other styling as needed */
}

.icon {
  /* Styling for your icon can go here */
  font-size: 1.5em;
  /* etc. */
}
--------------------------------------------------

In this structure, the .background-blur element is blurred, while the .content (which includes the 🔒 icon) remains sharp.

──────────────────────────────
Solution 2: Using a Pseudo‑Element

If you don’t want to change your HTML structure, you can use a pseudo‑element to create the blurred background:

--------------------------------------------------
HTML:
--------------------------------------------------
<div class="info-card locked-card" id="card7">
  <div class="title">Locked</div>
  <div class="value">99</div>
  <div class="icon">🔒</div>
</div>

--------------------------------------------------
CSS:
--------------------------------------------------
.locked-card {
  position: relative;
  overflow: hidden;
  /* Remove filters from here so that its children are not blurred */
  background: transparent;
}

.locked-card::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
  filter: blur(6px);
  z-index: 1; /* behind content */
}

/* Ensure that the card’s direct children (including icon) are rendered above the pseudo-element */
.locked-card > * {
  position: relative;
  z-index: 2;
}
--------------------------------------------------

This method creates a pseudo-element (::before) that covers the card area and has the blur applied. Since all direct children are given a higher stacking order, they appear sharp.

──────────────────────────────
Why Directly Applying Filter Doesn’t Work

If you were to simply add an icon inside the original element with this CSS…

--------------------------------------------------
HTML:
--------------------------------------------------
<div class="info-card locked-card" id="card7">
  <div class="title">Locked</div>
  <div class="value">99</div>
  <div class="icon">🔒</div>
</div>

--------------------------------------------------
CSS:
--------------------------------------------------
.locked-card {
  background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
  filter: blur(6px);
  position: relative;
}
--------------------------------------------------

…then the icon would be blurred as well because the blur filter is applied to the entire element and thus to all its child elements.

──────────────────────────────
Conclusion

To display a sharp icon on a blurred card background, you must separate the blurred effect from the content that should remain crisp. Either add a background element (or use a pseudo‑element) to carry the blurred style or restructure your HTML so that the content is in a container that isn’t affected by the blur filter.

Choose the solution that best fits your project, and you’ll have a nicely blurred background with a sharp locker icon on top!

