Below is one approach, but first a caveat: 

• In .NET you generally cannot “blit‐copy” a managed class like HashSet<string> because its memory layout isn’t sequential or fixed and it contains references (e.g. to strings and its internal arrays). 

• “Low‐level marshalling” (via, say, System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal or unsafe code) works only for blittable (unmanaged) types whose layout you control. HashSet<string> is neither.

Thus, if your goal is simply to turn a HashSet<string> into a byte array (and later reconstitute it) you will usually use a serialization mechanism. (A “true” low‐level marshalling that copies raw memory is unsafe, nonportable, and more likely to break with framework updates.)

Below is an example using BinaryFormatter (which works with HashSet<string> because it’s [Serializable]). Note that in .NET 5+ BinaryFormatter is discouraged for security reasons; you might consider a different serializer if that’s a concern. 

────────────────────────────
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;
using System.Runtime.Serialization.Formatters.Binary;

public static class HashSetSerializer
{
    public static byte[] Serialize(HashSet<string> hashSet)
    {
        if (hashSet == null)
            throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(hashSet));

        using (var ms = new MemoryStream())
        {
            var formatter = new BinaryFormatter();
            formatter.Serialize(ms, hashSet);
            return ms.ToArray();
        }
    }

    public static HashSet<string> Deserialize(byte[] buffer)
    {
        if (buffer == null)
            throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(buffer));

        using (var ms = new MemoryStream(buffer))
        {
            var formatter = new BinaryFormatter();
            return (HashSet<string>)formatter.Deserialize(ms);
        }
    }
}

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        var hs = new HashSet<string> { "apple", "banana", "cherry" };
        byte[] bytes = HashSetSerializer.Serialize(hs);
        Console.WriteLine("Marshalled to " + bytes.Length + " bytes.");

        var hs2 = HashSetSerializer.Deserialize(bytes);
        Console.WriteLine("Deserialized HashSet contains:");
        foreach (var s in hs2)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(s);
        }
    }
}
────────────────────────────

This code serializes the HashSet<string> into a byte array and then reconstitutes it.

If your intent is to actually copy raw memory (for example using unsafe code), then be aware that:

1. You cannot use Marshal.SizeOf or Marshal.StructureToPtr on HashSet<string> because it depends on internal implementation details of .NET.
2. The internal layout is subject to change across .NET versions.
3. Any “pointer copy” will include pointers into the managed heap, which aren’t valid outside the process or even later within the same process (and cannot be meaningfully reconstituted).

For these reasons, serialization is the recommended “marshal-like” approach for managed reference types.

If you have a scenario where you control the memory layout (for example a custom struct marked with [StructLayout(LayoutKind.Sequential)] that contains only unmanaged types), then you could use Marshal.StructureToPtr. But for HashSet<string>, a serializer is the best solution.

