The error

  "error CS0305: Using the generic type 'IList<T>' requires 1 type arguments"

occurs because of an ambiguity between the generic version of IList and its non-generic version. Let’s break it down:

──────────────────────────────
1. Why is Unity saying it is faulty?

When you write

  var list = (IList)obj;

the compiler is confused about which IList you mean. In .NET there are two common interfaces for lists:

 • System.Collections.IList – a non-generic interface
 • System.Collections.Generic.IList<T> – a generic interface that requires one type argument

Even though List<T> implements both, the code is using “IList” without a type parameter. In your using directives, if you’ve imported System.Collections.Generic, the compiler might be interpreting IList as System.Collections.Generic.IList<T> – and that interface needs a type argument (like IList<object> or IList<int>".) This is why you get the error message saying that “IList<T> requires 1 type argument.”

──────────────────────────────
2. Does it make sense that it is faulty? What is the logic behind the issue Unity is declaring?

Yes, it makes sense. The issue is not that your logic for iterating over the list is wrong; it’s purely a syntactical ambiguity and namespace conflict. Since you want to work generically with any list (and you’re only interested in its indices and values), you should be relying on the non-generic version of IList (System.Collections.IList) which is not subject to the same type parameter requirements.

The logic is:
 • List<> (a generic type) implements both the generic IList<T> and the non-generic IList.
 • When you don’t specify type parameters, the compiler ends up looking for the generic version, which is not legal.
 • To get around this, you need to explicitly refer to the non-generic one.

──────────────────────────────
3. The Functional Version and Its Motivation

To fix the problem, you should explicitly cast the object to System.Collections.IList rather than IList (which might be interpreted as the generic interface). Here’s the corrected code with a clear explanation:

-------------------------------------------------------------
private static Dictionary<string, object> SaveCollection(object obj)
{
    var collectionValues = new Dictionary<string, object>();
    var type = obj.GetType();

    if (type.IsArray)
    {
        var array = (Array)obj;
        for (int i = 0; i < array.Length; i++)
        {
            collectionValues[i.ToString()] = array.GetValue(i);
        }
    }
    else if (type.IsGenericType && type.GetGenericTypeDefinition() == typeof(List<>))
    {
        // Explicitly use the non-generic IList interface.
        var list = (System.Collections.IList)obj;
        for (int i = 0; i < list.Count; i++)
        {
            collectionValues[i.ToString()] = list[i];
        }
    }

    return collectionValues;
}
-------------------------------------------------------------

Motivation:
 • By explicitly writing System.Collections.IList, we remove the ambiguity.
 • The non-generic IList interface provides index-based access (Count and indexer) which is exactly what you need.
 • Unity (and the C# compiler) will now know exactly which interface you’re referring to, so the error disappears.

This solution ensures that your serialization function will work for any type of List (and other IList implementations), and Unity will accept it without error.

