response_a:
The problem is that hi.c never verifies that fopen succeeded. In hi.c, if "hi.txt" is missing or cannot be opened, fopen returns NULL. Then when fread is called with f (which is NULL), the program attempts to read from an invalid pointer, resulting in a segmentation fault. In contrast, newhi.c checks:

  if (f == NULL) { 
    perror("Error opening file"); 
    return 1; 
  }

which prevents the crash.

To fix hi.c, add an error check immediately after fopen:

  FILE *f = fopen("hi.txt", "r");
  if (f == NULL) {
    perror("Error opening file");
    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
  }

This will ensure you don’t try to read from a NULL pointer.

Below is an example of a single HTML file that uses embedded CSS and JavaScript to simulate a very basic operating system emulator. When you open the page you’ll see a “desktop” with two icons (File Explorer and Notepad). Clicking an icon opens a window that appears on the desktop. The file explorer lists a couple of sample files (held in a simple in‑memory file system), and clicking one opens it in a new window with a text area. Windows can be dragged by their title bars and closed with the “X” button. You can also click the corresponding icon on the taskbar (which appears at the bottom) to bring a window to the front.

