I'm using jira. Each of my jira issues has a description (e.g. {Field1:value1, Field2:value2, ...}) which should correspond to custom fields in the issue. I'm making a jira automation rule which should create a comment every time a custom value is changed. The following is a smart values jira automation comment using regex which should make a new comment with the new value and field if it doesn't already exist in the description:

{{#if(not(triggerIssue.fields.description.match(".*?fieldChange.field: fieldChange.toString.*?")))}}
{{triggerIssue.fields.description.match("\{(.*?)").concat(fieldChange.field+": "+fieldChange.toString)}}
{{/}}

This does not currently work. Please fix it.
Let's think step by step.

response_a:
Below is one working solution. The key points are to let Jira replace the smart value placeholders before doing the regex match and to escape characters properly. For example, instead of writing this:

  {{#if(not(triggerIssue.fields.description.match(".*?fieldChange.field: fieldChange.toString.*?")))}}
  {{triggerIssue.fields.description.match("\{(.*?)").concat(fieldChange.field+": "+fieldChange.toString)}}
  {{/}}

you can rework it (notice the use of interpolation for the smart values and the proper escaping of the “{” character) to something like this:

  {{#if(not(triggerIssue.fields.description.match(".*?{{fieldChange.field}}: {{fieldChange.toString}}.*?")))}}
  {{triggerIssue.fields.description.match("\\{(.*?)")}}{{fieldChange.field}}: {{fieldChange.toString}}
  {{/if}}

Here's what happens now step‐by‐step:
1. The if block tests whether the description already includes an entry for the field change. In the regex used in the match, the smart values {{fieldChange.field}} and {{fieldChange.toString}} are interpolated before the regex is applied.
2. The “{” is escaped (\\{) so that it is treated as a literal character.
3. If the regex evaluation returns false (the change isn’t in the description), the comment adds the current description content (using the regex match) and then appends the new field/value pair.

This version lets Jira replace the smart value placeholders inside the regex literals correctly and avoids the need for a concat call. (Depending on your needs you may adjust string handling if, for example, you’d prefer to format the comment differently.)

Give that a try in your automation rule.

