  memory                       = 512
  container_port               = 80
  host_port                    = 80
  cloudflare_token_ssm_path    = "/my/app/cloudflare-token"  # e.g. stored in SSM Parameter Store
  subnets                      = ["subnet-0123456789abcdef0", "subnet-0fedcba9876543210"]
  security_groups              = ["sg-0123456789abcdef0"]
  assign_public_ip             = true
  codecommit_repository_name   = "my-cloudflare-warp-repo"
  # pipeline_artifact_bucket_name can be omitted to let the module create one.
}

---------------------------------------------------------------

Notes & Next Steps

1. The CodePipeline in this example uses CodeCommit as its source; if you’d like to source your change triggers from elsewhere (for example, GitHub or triggering by an ECR image update), you can adjust the “Source” stage accordingly.
2. The CodeBuild project’s buildspec is very simple – it simply forces a new deployment of the ECS service when run. In practice you might want to add pre‑deployment tests or more complex logic.
3. The IAM policies here are intentionally broad for illustration purposes. In production you should scope the policies down according to the principle of least privilege.
4. Ensure that your AWS credentials and providers are correctly configured (including region settings) in your Terraform configuration.

This should provide you with a good starting point to deploy Cloudflare Warp into an ECS cluster via Terraform. Happy deploying!

