Below is an example of how to implement a singly linked list in C++ using smart pointers (specifically std::unique_ptr) so that memory is automatically reclaimed when nodes are no longer needed. The RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) principle built into C++ ensures that when an object goes out of scope, its destructor is automatically called, cleaning up any resources it owns. In this example, each node owns its successor via a unique_ptr, so when the node is destroyed, it recursively destroys its connected nodes.

────────────────────────────
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>

// Define the Node structure. Each node stores an integer and a unique_ptr to the next node.
struct Node {
    int data;
    std::unique_ptr<Node> next;

    // Constructor for creating a new Node
    Node(int value) : data(value), next(nullptr) {}
};

// The LinkedList class manages the list starting from a unique_ptr to the head
class LinkedList {
private:
    std::unique_ptr<Node> head; // The head pointer of the list

public:
    LinkedList() : head(nullptr) {}

    // Insert a new node at the front of the list
    void push_front(int value) {
        // Create a new node with the specified value
        auto new_node = std::make_unique<Node>(value);
        // The new node's next pointer takes ownership of the current head
        new_node->next = std::move(head);
        // Update the head pointer to the new node
        head = std::move(new_node);
    }

    // Print the contents of the list
    void print() const {
        Node* current = head.get();
        while (current != nullptr) {
            std::cout << current->data << " -> ";
            // Traverse using raw pointer access from unique_ptr
            current = current->next.get();
        }
        std::cout << "nullptr" << std::endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    // Create a linked list
    LinkedList list;

    // Add some elements to the list
    list.push_front(10);
    list.push_front(20);
    list.push_front(30);

    // Print the list's contents
    list.print();

    // When the list goes out of scope, all nodes are automatically reclaimed.
    return 0;
}
────────────────────────────

Explanation:

1. The Node struct contains a unique_ptr<Node> for the next node. This means that each node exclusively owns its successor.

2. The LinkedList class stores its head as a unique_ptr. When a new node is inserted at the front (via push_front), the new node takes ownership of the existing head.

3. When the LinkedList object is destroyed (e.g., when main() returns), the unique_ptr for head is destroyed. This destruction automatically calls the destructor of Node, which in turn destroys its own unique_ptr for the next node, recursively clearing the entire list.

This approach uses C++’s built-in resource management to “automatically” collect (garbage collect) the nodes when they are no longer needed—so manual delete calls are not necessary.

