Here's a JavaScript Events class that implements the required methods:

```javascript
class Events {
  constructor() {
    this.events = {};
  }

  /**
   * Subscribes a callback to an event
   * @param {string} eventName - Name of the event
   * @param {Function} callback - Function to call when event is triggered
   */
  subscribe(eventName, callback) {
    if (typeof eventName !== 'string') {
      throw new Error('Event name must be a string');
    }
    if (typeof callback !== 'function') {
      throw new Error('Callback must be a function');
    }

    if (!this.events[eventName]) {
      this.events[eventName] = [];
    }
    this.events[eventName].push(callback);
  }

  /**
   * Unsubscribes a callback from an event
   * @param {string} eventName - Name of the event
   * @param {Function} callback - Function to remove from subscriptions
   */
  unsubscribe(eventName, callback) {
    const callbacks = this.events[eventName];
    if (callbacks) {
      const index = callbacks.indexOf(callback);
      if (index !== -1) {
        callbacks.splice(index, 1);
      }
    }
  }

  /**
   * Triggers one or more events
   * @param {string|string[]} eventNames - Event(s) to trigger (array or single string)
   * @param  {...any} options - Arguments to pass to the callbacks
   */
  trigger(eventNames, ...options) {
    const eventsToTrigger = Array.isArray(eventNames) ? eventNames : [eventNames];

    eventsToTrigger.forEach(eventName => {
      const callbacks = this.events[eventName];
      if (callbacks) {
        // Make a copy to avoid mutation issues during iteration
        const callbacksCopy = callbacks.slice();
        for (const cb of callbacksCopy) {
          cb(...options);
        }
      }
    });
  }
}
```

**Example Usage:**
```javascript
const eventSystem = new Events();

// Subscribe to an event
const handler1 = (message) => console.log(`Handler 1 received: ${message}`);
const handler2 = (message) => console.log(`Handler 2 received: ${message}`);

eventSystem.subscribe('example', handler1);
eventSystem.subscribe('example', handler2);

// Trigger the event
eventSystem.trigger('example', 'Hello subscribers!'); 
// Output:
// Handler 1 received: Hello subscribers!
// Handler 2 received: Hello subscribers!

// Unsubscribe one handler
eventSystem.unsubscribe('example', handler1);

// Trigger again
eventSystem.trigger('example', 'Second message'); 
// Output:
// Handler 2 received: Second message

// Trigger multiple events
eventSystem.subscribe('another', () => console.log('Another event triggered'));
eventSystem.trigger(['example', 'another'], 'Multi trigger test');
// Output:
// Handler 2 received: Multi trigger test
// Another event triggered
```

**Key Features:**
1. Type checking for event names (must be strings) and callbacks (must be functions)
2. Can trigger single or multiple events in one call
3. Handles unsubscription properly by reference comparison
4. Uses defensive copying to avoid issues with array mutation during iteration
5. Supports passing any number of arguments to the event handlers

The class ensures that:
- Subscribers must use the exact callback function instance when unsubscribing
- Event handlers receive all arguments passed to the trigger method
- Unsubscribing during event triggering won't affect current event execution
- Multiple events can be triggered simultaneously in a single call

