Below is one way to refactor the test code by extracting the API operations into a dedicated service class (UserService) that adheres to the Single Responsibility Principle. In this design, the UserService “knows” only about making HTTP calls to create the user and update its permissions. The test itself remains focused on test orchestration and page interactions only. This approach also opens the door to reuse and easier mocking in the future.

Below is the refactored TypeScript code:

─────────────────────────────
// userService.ts

import { APIRequestContext } from '@playwright/test';

export interface CreateUserPayload {
  // Define properties for user creation
  name: string;
  email: string;
  // … other properties as needed
}

export interface PermissionPayload {
  // Define permission update properties (without the "id" property)
  role: string;
  // … other properties as needed
}

export interface AdminCredentials {
  username: string;
  password: string;
}

/**
 * This service is responsible for user-related API operations.
 * It adheres to SOLID principles by having one responsibility only.
 */
export class UserService {
  constructor(private readonly request: APIRequestContext) {}

  async createUser(createPayload: CreateUserPayload): Promise<{ id: string }> {
    const res = await this.request.post('/users/v2/create', { data: createPayload });
    if (res.status() !== 201) {
      throw new Error(`User creation failed with status: ${res.status()}`);
    }
    return await res.json();
  }

  async updatePermissions(userId: string, permissionPayload: PermissionPayload): Promise<AdminCredentials> {
    // Merge the user id with the permission payload
    const updatedPayload = { ...permissionPayload, id: userId };
    const res = await this.request.post('/users/v2/permissions', { data: updatedPayload });
    if (res.status() !== 200) {
      throw new Error(`Updating permissions failed with status: ${res.status()}`);
    }
    return await res.json();
  }

  /**
   * Creates an admin user and updates its permissions.
   * Returns the credentials used to login.
   */
  async createAdminUser(
    createPayload: CreateUserPayload,
    permissionPayload: PermissionPayload
  ): Promise<AdminCredentials> {
    const user = await this.createUser(createPayload);
    return await this.updatePermissions(user.id, permissionPayload);
  }
}

─────────────────────────────
// loginPage.ts

import { Page } from '@playwright/test';

/**
 * A Page Object representing the Login page.
 * Encapsulates the logic needed to perform the login action.
 */
export class LoginPage {
  constructor(private readonly page: Page) {}

  async login(username: string, password: string): Promise<void> {
    // Example selectors and interactions for login form. Adjust according to your app.
    await this.page.fill('#username', username);
    await this.page.fill('#password', password);
    await this.page.click('#loginButton');
  }
}

─────────────────────────────
// userAdmin.spec.ts

import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';
import { UserService, CreateUserPayload, PermissionPayload } from './userService';
import { LoginPage } from './loginPage';

test('create admin user and login', async ({ request, page }) => {
  // Prepare payloads as needed
  const createPayload: CreateUserPayload = {
    name: 'Admin User',
    email: 'admin@example.com',
    // … add other properties as necessary
  };

  const permissionPayload: PermissionPayload = {
    role: 'admin',
    // … add other properties as necessary
  };

  const userService = new UserService(request);

  // Create the admin user using the service
  const adminCredentials = await userService.createAdminUser(createPayload, permissionPayload);
  expect(adminCredentials.username).toBeDefined();
  expect(adminCredentials.password).toBeDefined();

  // Use a page object to perform the login step.
  const loginPage = new LoginPage(page);
  await loginPage.login(adminCredentials.username, adminCredentials.password);

  // Optionally add further assertions to verify the login success.
});
─────────────────────────────

Explanation:

1. The UserService class encapsulates all user-related API calls. It has a method createUser() to create a user resource and updatePermissions() to update the user's permissions. The createAdminUser() method orchestrates these steps.

2. The LoginPage class is a typical Page Object Model (POM) that contains the interactions needed for logging in.

3. The spec file (userAdmin.spec.ts) now only focuses on setting up the data, invoking the UserService, and using the page object to perform UI interactions. This separation helps you follow SOLID principles as well as makes the tests more readable and maintainable.

This design paves the way for more robust test maintenance and aligns with best practices in automation testing with Playwright and TypeScript.

