Below is one complete “from scratch” guide for setting up a NixOS system with flakes and Home Manager that includes GNOME running on Wayland, several editors (Neovim, VS Code, and Zed), the Brave browser, Bitwarden, plus many everyday tools (curl, wget, git, etc.). In this guide you’ll learn how to enable experimental features, create the required flake files (flake.nix, configuration.nix, and a Home Manager configuration), and finally rebuild your system. (You can substitute your own username, hostname, or tweak package selections as needed.)

─────────────────────────────  
Step 1. Enable Flakes in Nix

Before anything else, make sure your Nix install is ready for flakes. Edit (or create) the file /etc/nix/nix.conf and add the following line:

  experimental-features = nix-command flakes

This allows you to use the “nix flake” commands during development and rebuild.

─────────────────────────────  
Step 2. Create Your NixOS Configuration Directory

We’re going to put our flake files into the /etc/nixos directory (you may choose another directory for version control). In your shell as root you can do:

  cd /etc/nixos  
  mkdir -p my-flake  
  cd my-flake

Now you’ll create three files:
  • flake.nix  – the top-level flake that ties system and home-manager together  
  • configuration.nix – the NixOS system configuration (which sets up GNOME/Wayland, system packages, etc.)  
  • home.nix  – a Home Manager configuration for your user’s home environment

─────────────────────────────  
Step 3. Write Your flake.nix File

Below is a complete example for flake.nix. This flake pulls in nixpkgs (from unstable), Home Manager, and uses flake-utils to support multiple systems. (You can modify the "systems" list if you only care about one architecture.)

------------------------------------------------------------
{
  description = "My NixOS configuration with flakes and Home Manager";

  inputs = {
    nixpkgs.url = "github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-unstable";
    # Home Manager flake input (you can point this at a stable snapshot if you prefer)
    home-manager.url = "github:nix-community/home-manager";
    flake-utils.url = "github:numtide/flake-utils";
  };

  outputs = { self, nixpkgs, home-manager, flake-utils, ... }:
    flake-utils.lib.eachDefaultSystem (system: let
      pkgs = import nixpkgs {
        inherit system;
        config = { allowUnfree = true; };
      };

      # Import the Home Manager module
      hm-module = home-manager.lib.homeManagerConfiguration {
        inherit system;
        modules = [ ./home.nix ];
        # You can also include further Home Manager configuration here
      };
    in {
      nixosConfigurations = {
        # Replace "my-hostname" with your machine’s hostname 
        my-hostname = pkgs.lib.nixosSystem {
          inherit system;
          modules = [
            ./configuration.nix

            # Include Home Manager integration as a module:
            hm-module.nixosModule

            # Optional: any additional modules you want
          ];
          # Use specialArgs if you need to pass extra parameters
          specialArgs = { inherit hm-module; };
        };
      };
    });
}
------------------------------------------------------------

Save this file as /etc/nixos/my-flake/flake.nix.

─────────────────────────────  
Step 4. Write Your system Configuration (configuration.nix)

Here you configure core system settings. In our example we enable GNOME (for Wayland) through GDM, add system packages (including Neovim, VS Code, Zed, Brave, Bitwarden and some standard tools), and configure other necessary parts of your system.

Below is an example configuration.nix:

------------------------------------------------------------
{ config, pkgs, ... }:

{
  # Basic system info
  networking.hostName = "my-hostname";  # Change this to your desired hostname

  # Boot loader settings (assuming BIOS; adjust if using UEFI)
  boot.loader.grub.enable = true;
  boot.loader.grub.version = 2;

  # Enable GNOME with Wayland
  services.xserver = {
    enable = true;
    layout = "us";  # Adjust keyboard layout as needed
    # Use GDM as the display manager (GDM automatically defaults to Wayland if possible)
    displayManager.gdm.enable = true;
    displayManager.gdm.wayland = true;

    # Enable the GNOME desktop environment
    desktopManager.gnome3.enable = true;

    # Also enable the Wayland support
    wayland.enable = true;
  };

  # System-wide packages. Here we add our everyday tools and applications:
  environment.systemPackages = with pkgs; [
    curl             # Command-line tool
    wget             # File retrieval tool
    git              # Version control
    htop             # Process viewer
    make             # Build tool
    gcc              # Compiler (C/C++)
    gdb              # Debugger
    tree             # Display directory trees
    unzip            # Archive utility
    zip              # Archive utility
    tmux             # Terminal multiplexer
    python3          # Python interpreter

    # Applications
    neovim           # Neovim editor
    vscode           # Visual Studio Code (official package; sometimes named "vscode" or "code" in nixpkgs)
    zed              # Zed editor (ensure it is available in your nixpkgs channel or overlay)
    brave            # Brave browser
    bitwarden        # Bitwarden client (or use an unofficial wrapper if necessary)
  ];

  # Optionally adjust locales, time settings, etc.
  time.timeZone = "UTC";

  # You may add firewall or other service configurations as needed
}
------------------------------------------------------------

Save this as /etc/nixos/my-flake/configuration.nix.

─────────────────────────────  
Step 5. Write Your Home Manager Configuration (home.nix)

Here you configure your user environment. In this example, we’ll configure Neovim settings (plus optionally extend it with plugins if you like) and add any other per-user packages or settings. (Note that VS Code, Zed, Brave, etc. are provided system-wide in the configuration above, but you can also manage per-user options if desired.)

------------------------------------------------------------
{ config, pkgs, ... }:

{
  # Change "testUser" to your actual username.
  home.username = "testUser";
  home.homeDirectory = "/home/testUser";

  # Example: Configure Neovim
  programs.neovim = {
    enable = true;
    viAlias = true;
    # Optionally add plugins here; for instance, if you have a custom vim plugin set:
    # plugins = with pkgs.vimPlugins; [
    #   vim-airline vim-fugitive
    # ];
  };

  # You can also install per-user packages (in addition to the system ones)
  home.packages = with pkgs; [
    # For instance, nodejs if you need development dependencies
    nodejs
  ];

  # Additional Home Manager settings can go here. For example, you could set up
  # custom shell configuration or dconf settings for GNOME.
}
------------------------------------------------------------

Save this as /etc/nixos/my-flake/home.nix.

─────────────────────────────  
Step 6. Rebuild and Switch to the New Configuration

Now that you have created your flake, it’s time to switch to your new system configuration. Run the following command as root:

  nixos-rebuild switch --flake /etc/nixos/my-flake#my-hostname

This command tells NixOS to rebuild your system using the flake defined at /etc/nixos/my-flake and to apply the configuration for the “my-hostname” system. (Make sure you use the same hostname as defined in your configuration.nix.)

─────────────────────────────  
Step 7. Enjoy Your New Setup!

Once the rebuild finishes and your system restarts (if necessary), you will have:

• GNOME running on Wayland (managed by GDM)  
• A complete system set of packages including curl, wget, git, htop, make, gcc, gdb, tree, unzip, zip, and tmux  
• Development editors: Neovim, Visual Studio Code, and Zed  
• Applications: Brave and Bitwarden  
• A personalized user environment via Home Manager

If you need to make updates, edit your flake files and run the rebuild command again. You can also experiment with additional Home Manager modules and system modules to further customize your environment.

─────────────────────────────  
Additional Tips

• Always back up your current configuration before making big changes.  
• If a package (like “zed” or “bitwarden”) is not available in the upstream nixpkgs, consider adding an overlay or using an alternative package name.  
