Below is one way to implement the function. In this solution we assume that “closest or equal to budget” means that out of all possible pairs (one element from arr1 and one element from arr2) we want the one whose sum is as close as possible to b without going over (so that if an exact match exists, that is chosen). If no pair can be made without exceeding b, we return –1. (If you prefer a different behavior for that case, adjust accordingly.) 

For example:

------------------------------------------------
function getExpense(arr1, arr2, b) {
  // Initialize best as -1 to indicate if no valid pair is found.
  let best = -1;

  // Check every combination of numbers from arr1 and arr2.
  for (let i = 0; i < arr1.length; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < arr2.length; j++) {
      const sum = arr1[i] + arr2[j];

      // If the sum is less than or equal to budget and better than our current best, update best.
      if (sum <= b && sum > best) {
        best = sum;

        // Optional: if we've hit the budget exactly, we can return right away.
        if (best === b) {
          return best;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  return best;
}

// Example usage:
const arr1 = [40, 50, 60];
const arr2 = [5, 8, 12];
const b = 58;
console.log(getExpense(arr1, arr2, b)); // Output will be the pair sum closest or equal to 58.
------------------------------------------------

Explanation:
1. We initialize best to -1 in case no pair can be found that doesn’t exceed the budget.
2. We iterate over each element in arr1 and arr2 and compute their sum.
3. If the sum is within budget (i.e. ≤ b) and larger than our current best, we update the best.
4. If we hit exactly b, we return immediately since that’s the best possible match.
5. Finally, we return the best sum found (or –1 if no valid pair exists).

This solution runs in O(n * m) time where n and m are the lengths of arr1 and arr2 respectively, which is perfectly acceptable for arrays of moderate size.

